What you’re looking at
DriftScope is a small modular stack that answers one question repeatedly: given an ocean and a release point, where does the stuff go? “Stuff” is sediment, plastic, larvae, an oil slick, a search-and-rescue target — anything that moves with the surface flow. The whole project is one equation:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{X}}{dt}
= \underbrace{\mathbf{u}_\text{currents}}_{\text{CMEMS / HYCOM}}
+ \underbrace{\mathbf{u}_\text{Stokes}}_{\text{ERA5 waves}}
+ \underbrace{\mathbf{u}_\text{tides}}_{\text{TPXO9}}
+ \underbrace{\alpha\,\mathbf{u}_{10}}_{\text{ERA5 winds}}
+ \underbrace{\mathbf{w}_\text{settle}}_{\text{Stokes' law}}
+ \underbrace{\boldsymbol{\eta}_t}_{\text{Brownian}}
\tag{1}\]
Eq. 1 is the spine. Each term is one chapter in the Physics part of this book; each is one self-contained module under driftscope/. The architecture choice is: build the spine, bolt on terms.

6.6 Land, NaN, and out-of-bounds
A particle that wanders into a coast cell samples a NaN. Parcels’ default is to raise. That makes coastal regions unworkable. DriftScope takes a gentler approach: a tiny extra kernel DeleteOOB (simulate.py:381) runs after AdvectionRK4 and removes any particle that has gone out of bounds.
def DeleteOOB(particle, fieldset, time):
    if particle.state == StatusCode.ErrorOutOfBounds:
        particle.delete()
The result is what you want: particles that drift toward shore disappear (beached) instead of crashing the whole run. The trajectory file just has fewer rows in later time steps; pandas/xarray handle this transparently.
